    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11540-026-10035-w",
    "abstract": "Abstract The frequency of high-intensity (extreme) rainfall is expected to increase due to climate change. At local-scale impact on potato yields can be large, with great effects on farmers\u2019 income. If the event occurs at a larger scale, affecting many farms in the same region at the same time, then also the processing industry is affected, causing rising prices and costly reshuffling of supply chains. Previous studies showed a prolonged dry summer and a prolonged wet autumn are large-scale weather extremes causing large yield losses, not only at farm scale but also at provincial and national level. For high-intensity (extreme) rainfall the scale of the events and the scale of the impact was to date unclear. Previous studies showed large impacts on Dutch potato yields at field and farm scale. The current study shows in the past 31 years in 12 Dutch provinces high-intensity (extreme) rainfall occurred only at a local scale, causing strong local effects (previous studies) but no large scale yield anomalies (this study).",
    "field": "Agricultural meteorology",
    "problem": "Existing studies offer contradictory conclusions on high-intensity rainfall's impact on Dutch potato yields, with local-scale analyses showing significant damage while larger, provincial-scale studies find no effect, a discrepancy this paper attributes to the localized nature of such rainfall events.",
    "method": "The author statistically assesses the co-occurrence of high-intensity rainfall and potato yield extremes using 31 years of Dutch provincial-scale data to test the hypothesis that rainfall impact is scale-dependent and averages out at larger scales.",
    "task": "The task is to assess the spatial scale of high-intensity rainfall and its impact on Dutch potato yields to resolve contradictory findings from previous local-scale and large-scale studies.",
